  "description": {
   "content": [
    {
     "content": [
      {
       "text": "Noting FCREPO-761, it is currently possible to specify resources in a policy to using regular expressions.",
       "type": "text"
      }
     ],
     "type": "paragraph"
    },
    {
     "content": [
      {
       "text": "Indexing/locating policies based on regular expression syntax will be difficult (if not impossible) given the potential range of regular expressions that could be used to specify resources.  Currently only a small subset of regular expressions are searched for.",
       "type": "text"
      }
     ],
     "type": "paragraph"
    },
    {
     "content": [
      {
       "text": "Instead of using the anyURI-regexp-match in conjunction with hierarchical URIs, it would be better to specify a set of ResourceAttributeDesignators that do not use regular expressions and are therefore (a) more semtically precise and (b) much easier to index and search for.",
       "type": "text"
      }
     ],
     "type": "paragraph"
    },
    {
     "content": [
      {
       "text": "A full set of use-cases would need defining, but the following could be candidates",
       "type": "text"
      }
     ],
     "type": "paragraph"
    },
    {
     "content": [
      {
       "content": [
        {
         "content": [
          {
           "text": "all children of resource xyz (ie immediate collection members)",
           "type": "text"
          }
         ],
         "type": "paragraph"
        }
       ],
       "type": "listItem"
      },
      {
       "content": [
        {
         "content": [
          {
           "text": "all descendants of resource xyz (ie resources that are members of collections that are also members of xyz)",
           "type": "text"
          }
         ],
         "type": "paragraph"
        }
       ],
       "type": "listItem"
      }
     ],
     "type": "bulletList"
    },
    {
     "content": [
      {
       "text": "As part of this we should also consider the requirements of FCREPO-636, ie a policy object applies to a collection xyz through an \"appliesTo\"; a policy object applies to collection members through \"appliesToChildrenOf\"",
       "type": "text"
      }
     ],
     "type": "paragraph"
    }
   ],
   "type": "doc",
   "version": 1
  },
